You get melee combat whether you like it or not. It doesn't need to be
advocated. Jove, actually, has been as good about melee combat as it
demands; he accurately stated the damages, using the maximally effective
approaches (twoweapon, Grayswandir, +7 weapons, etc); accurately stated
the threat zone (eight squares); accurately stated the vulnerability to
reprisals (more).

>>You mean, if nothing bad happens. Try tickling Demogorgon to death like
>>that some time?
>Demongorgon is a threat, so it doesn't count under the "safely can skip
>ranged combat".

So, actually, your objection is non-existent; you want ranged combat to be
used on threats.

>>This analysis would be true in some strange alternative universe where all
>>the monsters started exactly the same distance away from you. They don't.
>I don't understand why you think this requires monsters to start
>exactly the same distance.  It doesn't.

Because in your fantasy scenario, I arrive in the Mines, start plinking
away with ranged attacks (which in this case, we imagine, are less
effective than the character's melee attacks), and suddenly I'm
surrounded all at once. That doesn't, in fact, happen - unless the things
surrounding me all started the same distance away. They arrive piecemeal
and can be swatted down with my powerful melee attack - if it _is_ more
powerful, which it might well be, I'm going to use it when a monster is in
melee range.

>So here comes the ranting part.  I really don't understand why some
>people feel the need to completely overkill the monsters.  Seriously,
>do some people really feel the need to blast everything with Magic
>Missile at XL30, when the game is easy enough to beat with Magic
>Missile at XL14?

This question's nothing to do with the issue here. The question is about
advice to people who want to die less. You die to monsters if you haven't
killed them first; two approaches here are good tactics to protect
yourself from reprisals, such as fighting in choke points (which no-one is
discarding), and ... killing monsters faster.

The real question would be, what to tell someone who uses Magic Missile at
XL14 and still gets killed by monsters? _They_ should feel the need to -
perhaps amongst other things - increase their XL and do more damage.

>Do some people really feel the need to boost their HP
>and Pw rediculously high, even though it's completely unnecessary,

Maybe they do, but again that's not the question. The question is; what
advice to give someone who runs out of HP and PW? One approach to that
problem is... techniques for increasing HP and PW.

>A Melee-heavy strategy is completely valid for a melee character.  This
>really isn't a "special conduct".

Straw man; no-one said it was.

>Just because you want to beat the
>game with minimal effort doesn't mean that everyone else should be
>using a ranged-heavy strategy just because it has a lower risk.

This is the perfect expression of this fallacy of playing a suboptimal
strategy because it's "normal". The question being asked is how to win.
"Lower risk" is exactly what we're looking for.

>And I daresay you must be terrible at melee combat if you think
>melee-heavy is necessarily bad.

I wondered when we'd get around to this sort of cheap shot. At the point
you first ascended, October 2004, I had 16 ascensions under my belt, the
first of which was in 1995. At my best, I've ascended 4 games in nine
consecutive ones; rogue, wizard, tourist, and healer. This doesn't make me
Marvin or Rast, but it's not too shabby, I think. And, of course, all of
this (and almost all my ascensions) were before Jove produced his insights
into ranged combat; many of them were before multifire, weapon skills, and
spell skills gave ranged combat the benefits it has. So I did them with
melee.
